  "narrative": "Compared to last sale (11-10-26): Yearling steers and heifers were too lightly tested to develop an accurate market trend, however higher undertones were noticed on both sexes. Steers calves under 450 lbs were too lightly tested to develop an accurate market trend, however higher undertones were noticed. 450-549 lbs steers sold mostly 12.00-15.00 higher; 550-649 lbs steers sold 5.00-10.00 higher; steer calves over 650 lbs sold generally steady. Heifer calves under 400 lbs were too lightly tested to develop an accurate market trend, however higher undertones were noticed. 400-649 lbs heifers sold mostly 5.00-8.00 higher. Heifer calves over 650 lbs were too lightly tested last week to develop an accurate market trend, however higher undertones were noticed. Quality this week was mostly average to attractive. A few larger consignments off reputation ranches continue to be seen. Most of these offerings were never offered on the summer video, giving buyers a shot at every calf in their 2026 calf crop. These offerings were met with very good demand. Many cutback calves off summer video sales were seen as well. These offerings were met with moderate to good demand. Several ranchers were in the stands this week looking for high quality replacement heifers. Heifers all sold higher as feeding buyers and replacement buyers bid aggressively over high quality offerings. Light weight cattle of both sexes suitable to run on grass and develop into yearlings sold on very good demand. CME positions sold off today with the January and later contracts selling 5.00-6.50 lower, however buyers showed resilience as they bid on feeder cattle and pushed all offerings higher. Market activity this week was mostly active with a large pool of buyers noticed this week. Weigh up cows sold on mostly light to moderate demand for moderate to heavy offerings. Feeding cow demand was mostly light to moderate this week. Feeding cows sold mostly steady to firm on boning flesh cows, lean fleshed cows sold steady to 3.00 higher with more buyers in the market this week. Canadian buyers were in the market again this week, these buyers quickly filled loads and exited the market. American packers continue to purchase a few cows to put away for the holiday. Most of these offerings were slightly lame or blemished in some way and purchased at slaughter cow price. Slaughter cows sold mostly steady to weak on all classes. Cows offered buyers very good weigh conditions this sale with many very empty offerings noticed. Slaughter bulls sold generally steady in a narrow comparison. Young age cows sold on moderate demand this week, but lower demand than was seen last week. Quality was mostly average this week. These offerings sold mostly 5.00-10.00 lower on a lower quality offering. A few exposed older cows sold today these are located on the report as stock cows below. These cows were not tested at the ranch or at the yards. These offerings sold on mostly good demand. Supply included: 75% Feeder Catt",
